<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurotas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Evrotas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eurotas is the main river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> into the Laconian Gulf. Its fertile valley was the heart of the Mycenaean kingdom remembered as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> and later of the Spartan state. In antiquity it was linked with many myths and cults, among them those of Helen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eurotas.jpg" alt="Eurotas" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: vaggelis vlahos · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurotas_evlahos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sparta lay in the valley of the <b>Eurotas River</b> in Laconia, in the south-eastern Peloponnese, between the Taygetus and Parnon mountain ranges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Eurotas</b> valley of Laconia, Mycenaean elites appear from c. 1500 BCE, as shown by the rich tholos tomb of Vapheio with its gold cups.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-laconia">Mycenaean Kingdom of Laconia</a></li></ul></section>
